<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="phloridzin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6515" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD-12447 Phlorizin| is a glucoside of |FRAME: PHLORETIN|, a dihydrochalcone |CITS: [20356611]|. |FRAME: CPD-12447 Phlorizin| was first discovered in 1835 as a bitter tasting compound in the bark of apple trees (|FRAME: TAX-3750|). Tissue distribution of the compound differs considerably among species; in |FRAME: TAX-283210| (paradise apple) and Lithocarpus polystachyus it mainly exists in fruits, in |FRAME: TAX-106556| it is found in the stem and root barks, young leaves and fruits. The root bark contained more of it than the stem bark |CITS: [20410591]|. In |FRAME: TAX-3750| it comprises 90% of the soluble phenolics in the leaves. This compound has been used for medicinal properties for a long time. In modern medicine it is used in the control of blood sugar levels and as a protective agent to the heart from scavenging oxygen free radicals and antioxidant injuries. Studies have also shown that |FRAME: CPD-12447| has estrogenic and antiestrogenic activities |CITS: [ 20410591]|. Dihydrochalcones are related to flavonoids but much less is known about their biosynthetic route. The pathway depends on the presence of a dehydrogenase that can form |FRAME: CPD-12449| from |FRAME: P-COUMAROYL-COA|. No |FRAME: CPD-12447| biosynthesis occurs in |FRAME: TAX-23211| (pear), which is closely related to apples, due to the absence of the enzyme that catalyzes the first step |CITS: [IND44141307]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
